## First-In-First-Out with Queue

A **queue** is a FIFO (First In, First Out) data structure. Items are added to the back and removed from the front — like a line of people.

### Building a Queue with Vec

```xiom
pub type Queue[T] = { items: Vec[T]; }

fn Queue.new[T]() -> Queue[T] {
  return Queue{ items: Vec[T]() };
}

fn Queue.enqueue[T](self: &mut Queue[T], item: T) -> Unit {
  self.items.push(item);  // add to back
}

fn Queue.dequeue[T](self: &mut Queue[T]) -> Option[T] {
  if self.items.len() == 0 { return None; }
  let first = self.items[0];
  self.items.remove(0);  // remove from front (O(n) with Vec)
  return Some(first);
}

fn Queue.peek[T](self: &Queue[T]) -> Option[T] {
  if self.items.len() == 0 { return None; }
  return Some(self.items[0]);
}
```

### Using a Queue

```xiom
var q = Queue[Int].new();
q.enqueue(1);
q.enqueue(2);
q.enqueue(3);  // queue: [1, 2, 3] — front is 1

let front = q.peek();    // Some(1)
let val = q.dequeue();   // Some(1)
let val2 = q.dequeue();  // Some(2)
```

### FIFO Behavior

```
enqueue(1):  [1]            front=1
enqueue(2):  [1, 2]         front=1
enqueue(3):  [1, 2, 3]      front=1
dequeue():   [2, 3]    → 1
dequeue():   [3]       → 2
dequeue():   []        → 3
```

### Better Queue with LinkedList

For better O(1) dequeue, use `LinkedList` instead of `Vec`:

```xiom
pub type FastQueue[T] = { items: LinkedList[T]; }

fn FastQueue.dequeue[T](self: &mut FastQueue[T]) -> Option[T] {
  return self.items.pop_front();  // O(1) with LinkedList!
}
```

### Practical Uses

- **Task scheduling**: process jobs in order received
- **Print queue**: first document sent prints first
- **Breadth-first search**: explore nearest neighbors first
- **Message passing**: deliver messages in order
- **Buffering**: streaming data processing

